WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.440
Adobe had a stranglehold on video editing.&nbsp;

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:08.480
Premiere Pro. After Effects.
$69 a month. Subscription only.&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.200
Giant cancellation fees.
It’s been the way for years.&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.560
But that's changing.
People are starting to ditch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.120
Premiere Pro, for something else.
A software with no subscription,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:26.480
that you buy once and own forever.
Which has a free version, with 95%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:29.280
of the features.
Davinci Resolve.&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:34.160
The software that dominates TV and&nbsp;
Hollywood, is now coming for Adobe.&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:48.640
This is how DaVinci Resolve&nbsp;
beat Adobe at its own game.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.440
What some people miss is that DaVinci&nbsp;
isn't really just "another editor".&nbsp;

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.680
It has something very powerful,&nbsp;
but also very specific.&nbsp;

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:02.400
To understand why DaVinci is so important,&nbsp;
we actually have to look where it's actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.520
used the most.
Hollywood movies.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:09.440
Imagine the movie process as&nbsp;
lots of overlapping pipes.&nbsp;

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:13.600
There's the rough, basic edit, using&nbsp;
proxy files: aka lower quality versions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.080
of footage. This is often done with&nbsp;
Avid Media Composer, or Premiere Pro.&nbsp;

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:24.480
Then it splits into different pipes:
There's VFX, which is a gigantic part with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.200
all kinds of artists, tools, and steps.
Then, there's the conform stage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.760
where that low quality is upgraded&nbsp;
to the real, high-resolution media.&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.360
There's sound design and&nbsp;
mixing, often with composing.&nbsp;

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:42.400
And then, there's color grading.
So where does DaVinci fit?&nbsp;

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.840
Right here (point to color grading).
Color grading. Color correction, like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:51.680
fixing inconsistent lighting and exposure, and&nbsp;
grading itself, the tone and palette of the movie.&nbsp;

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:58.640
DaVinci could handle a lot of these steps, but&nbsp;
it is hands down, undeniably the best at this.&nbsp;

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:04.400
This isn't a small niche audience either.
Most major movies, TV commercials, TV shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:11.280
and music videos are color graded in Resolve.
Star Wars, Avatar, The Martian, Jason Bourne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:16.560
Oppenheimer, Superman, Alien Romulus, Pirates&nbsp;
of the Caribbean… it'd probably be easier to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:23.360
list blockbusters not color-graded in DaVinci.
This is actually where one of their problems was.&nbsp;

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:28.960
You see, DaVinci isn't new. It's&nbsp;
actually been around for over 40 years.&nbsp;

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:33.600
And through the 80s and 90s, DaVinci&nbsp;
had built a reputation as the gold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.360
standard for color grading.
(show below products on screen):&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:39.280
DaVinci Classic, DaVinci&nbsp;
Renaissance, and DaVinci 2K.&nbsp;

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:47.200
But, it was a bit different back then.
These products were between $200,000 and $800,000.&nbsp;

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:52.160
Not cheap, but this was pretty&nbsp;
standard for filmmaking tools.&nbsp;

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:56.720
It wasn't you or me buying this&nbsp;
software, it was giant studios.&nbsp;

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:03.040
This continued for decades, then out of&nbsp;
nowhere, seemed to come the end of DaVinci.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.120
The corporate owner, Acterna,&nbsp;
went bankrupt in 2008.&nbsp;

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:10.800
But… DaVinci, the company within&nbsp;
Acterna, was still profitable.&nbsp;

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.160
Though that didn't matter.
Even if part of a business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:19.840
is profitable, when the whole thing goes&nbsp;
into bankruptcy, it can be very messy.&nbsp;

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:25.040
But ironically, this was probably&nbsp;
the best thing to happen to DaVinci.&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.400
Different assets went up for&nbsp;
sale at a bankruptcy auction.&nbsp;

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:32.560
DaVinci was bought by the Australian&nbsp;
camera company Blackmagic Design.&nbsp;

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.960
And more specifically, its owner Grant Petty.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:41.680
His company was a bit different.
It was very self sufficient. They hadn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:45.840
borrowed money and had basically no investment.
[Grant Petty, BMD]: "If you're using venture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.200
capital, you're always desperate&nbsp;
to pay back the investment"&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.240
"[We were] in the right place and fit enough to&nbsp;
take the opportunity. "Sales were up [for BMD's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:59.200
products] and we were doing quite well".
The price for DaVinci was $10 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:04.880
which was quite a bargain, since buying&nbsp;
the software itself cost $250,000.&nbsp;

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:10.080
But Grant had big plans, which would&nbsp;
change the world of video forever.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:38.080
These VPNs managed to grab highly confidential&nbsp;
information like salaries and client payments,

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.240
some even had their bank accounts emptied.&nbsp;

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.420
If you’re a freelancer or run a business,&nbsp;
putting financial information in lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:42.960
scattered, unknown tools, isn’t the best idea.
And for something that important, it’s better to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:45.920
be safe than sorry.
That’s where Xero can help.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:49.440
Xero is a cloud-based accounting&nbsp;
platform that helps small businesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:54.320
manage finances: with invoicing,&nbsp;
bills, payments, payroll, sales tax,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:57.825
and more all in one secure location.
Xero encrypts your business information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.825 --> 00:04:57.895
has multi-factor authentication, and&nbsp;
24/7 monitoring and surveillance.&nbsp;

00:04:57.895 --> 00:04:57.995
They even have a security noticeboard to keep you&nbsp;
informed about recent scams and how to avoid them.

00:04:57.995 --> 00:05:00.880
Xero helps with one of the&nbsp;
hardest parts of freelancing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:24.556
which is surprisingly charging people.
Following up if clients forget can be awkward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.556 --> 00:05:28.800
and what if they use a different payment method&nbsp;
others didn’t? What about different currencies?&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.560
Xero gives your invoices a “pay now”&nbsp;
button, which makes paying you easy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:38.880
and accepts online payments, credit&nbsp;
cards, even Apple &amp; Google Pay.&nbsp;

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:45.280
Xero can automatically send clients reminders too.
Xero will also help you easily prepare and file&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:49.200
taxes, removing stress from one&nbsp;
of the busiest times of the year.

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:53.840
Xero have all kinds of plans. Whether you&nbsp;
run a small business, are a freelancer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:58.000
or just have a side-gig, Xero&nbsp;
has the perfect plan for you.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.040
As of the time of making&nbsp;
this video, Xero is offering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:07.600
90% off for 6 months using the link below.
Join 4.6 million subscribers, and start using Xero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:12.960
to get on top of your finances, and your privacy.
Thank you to Xero for sponsoring our videos.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:17.100
Even though Blackmagic was doing&nbsp;
well, it was time to change things up.&nbsp;

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:19.760
[Grant Petty, BMD]: "Other companies were&nbsp;
stuck in the world of charging a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:25.600
money for their products but we weren't playing&nbsp;
by the same rules. Resolve was a good product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:31.120
but it was the same old hardware, so&nbsp;
we decided to focus on the software."&nbsp;

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:35.680
Grant wanted to change things up, and&nbsp;
the key to that was DaVinci Resolve.&nbsp;

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:40.400
So, in 2010, at the National&nbsp;
Association of Broadcasters show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:47.280
Grant and Blackmagic made a big announcement.
New versions, and more importantly, a new price:&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:52.320
$995 for a macOS version.
$29,995 for the advanced macOS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:58.160
and $19,995 for the advanced Linux version.
That might sound like a lot. But remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:02.080
once you bought it, you owned it.
Highly robust, top-of-the-line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:05.440
tools for Hollywood movies.
And before this, they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:11.440
between $200,000 and $800,000.
This was a huge change.&nbsp;

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:16.320
But right after, the entire industry&nbsp;
began to go through a metamorphosis.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.320
In 2013, Adobe dropped the&nbsp;
"pay once and own it" model.&nbsp;

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:24.080
Now, everything would be bundled&nbsp;
under one big monthly payment.&nbsp;

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:26.000
Creative Cloud.
I don't think I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:30.960
need to explain why Adobe is so annoying.
Deceptive cancellation fees, a short trial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:37.520
a giant monthly price tag, the FTC lawsuit.
But, the money doesn't lie.

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:41.680
Other companies began to jump on.
And soon every piece of software you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:46.320
could imagine moved to subscription.
So, what about Blackmagic?&nbsp;

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:50.240
Grant could have swapped to a subscription&nbsp;
license here, and made a lot of money.&nbsp;

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:55.920
But, he essentially did the opposite to Adobe.
Grant has made it clear how much he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:58.160
despises these plans.
[Grant Petty]: "Cloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:03.920
licensors are like slumlords. You have to keep&nbsp;
buying from them and the more you're loyal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:39.920
the more you'll get penalized. It is like your dog&nbsp;
does something nice and you beat it with a stick."

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:02.240
DaVinci moved in the other direction.
Not only did they stay a "pay once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:08.640
and own" model, but they doubled down.
In 2017, with the release of DaVinci Resolve 14,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:12.320
the price dropped even more.
To just $299.&nbsp;

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:15.440
And, this wasn't a cheap, basic version either.&nbsp;

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:18.880
This was the full package,&nbsp;
with even more features.&nbsp;

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:22.880
Notably, a full scale video&nbsp;
editor. Like Premiere Pro.&nbsp;

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:29.760
Grant also said "The full DaVinci Resolve&nbsp;
14 Studio is now available for only $299,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:34.640
which is less than the cost of most&nbsp;
annual cloud-based subscription plans"&nbsp;

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:40.800
I think we all know who he's talking about.
But, somehow, that wasn't even the biggest change.&nbsp;

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:43.920
Today, if you go to DaVinci&nbsp;
Resolve's homepage, you'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:51.120
see two buttons: Free Download, and Buy now.
So, what is that free download? A free trial?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:53.760
A super basic DaVinci?
No.&nbsp;

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:57.840
The free version has about&nbsp;
95% of the paid product.&nbsp;

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:02.480
I'm not kidding, you can go try&nbsp;
it right now. It's unbelievable.&nbsp;

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:06.400
They don't even ask for your credit&nbsp;
card. Just a few details and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:11.600
they ask you what features they should add.
The only things it doesn't have are resolutions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:17.200
beyond 4k, collaboration tools, and a few&nbsp;
other features used by industry professionals.&nbsp;

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:22.240
It reminds me of software from the 2000s.&nbsp;
"Here you go. We hope you like it. Anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:25.680
we can do to improve?"
And that's not all.&nbsp;

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:31.280
Blackmagic just kept adding stuff to DaVinci.
In 2017 they acquired Fairlight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:35.120
an audio editing software.
Then added it to DaVinci Resolve.&nbsp;

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:42.240
Then in 2018, they added VFX and composition tools&nbsp;
from Fusion, which they had acquired back in 2014.

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:47.920
DaVinci just kept getting better and better.
Soon this software had everything.&nbsp;

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:53.680
Not only the best color-grading you could get, far&nbsp;
better than anything Adobe had, but a great video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:59.520
editor, VFX tools, and audio editing.
And all for that $299 price tag,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:03.440
and a robust free version.
More and more people were beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:07.680
to look over from Premiere Pro.
But, why do this?&nbsp;

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:13.520
Why give away most of your product for free?
Surely this makes no business sense, right?&nbsp;

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:17.360
Well, it actually made much&nbsp;
more sense than you might think.&nbsp;

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:33.920
And even if it was still in the background, word&nbsp;
of DaVinci Resolve was beginning to get around…

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:39.920
All kinds of fascinating business stories happen&nbsp;
all the time, some without anyone noticing.&nbsp;

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:44.000
And they have heaps of things to teach us.
We cover lots of these stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:50.800
so subscribe! We're almost at 1&nbsp;
million, and you can help us get there.

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:54.480
For DaVinci, there are two&nbsp;
important factors at play.&nbsp;

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:58.240
First, Blackmagic isn't a&nbsp;
software company, like Adobe.&nbsp;

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:01.120
They're still a camera and industry tool company.

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:05.280
Highly advanced, professional&nbsp;
tools like cameras around $30,000.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:09.760
Editing dashboards, color panels, all&nbsp;
for hundreds or thousands of dollars.&nbsp;

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:13.920
Their gigantic consoles for movie&nbsp;
studios go for tens of thousands.

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:17.280
Blackmagic isn't a software company like Adobe.&nbsp;

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:22.960
They're a hardware company, and DaVinci&nbsp;
Resolve is the software that goes alongside it.&nbsp;

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:27.600
So why does this matter?
Because of the second factor.

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:31.520
Making most of it free, and the full&nbsp;
version a "buy and own forever" license,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:34.480
aligns with Grant's philosophy.
[Grant Petty]: " I think the computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:38.960
industry has forgotten about the spirit&nbsp;
behind when they were setup. Personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:44.560
computers were about distributing computing&nbsp;
power to individual people. Now it's become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:50.720
a centralization of your data and you're the&nbsp;
product, not the product you're actually buying.&nbsp;

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:56.880
I think the computer industry has lost its way."
That point about "distributing computing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.880 --> 00:13:02.400
power to individual people" is key.
Grant calls this "democratizing creativity".&nbsp;

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:06.960
It makes it easier for people to jump&nbsp;
in, create something, and maybe end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:13.680
on a path to being a filmmaker, freelance editor,&nbsp;
color-grader, audio engineer, or even a YouTuber!&nbsp;

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:18.400
Those who start in DaVinci may, down the&nbsp;
road, as they go from amateur to professional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:23.440
pay for the other products by Blackmagic.
Plus, it does something very smart.&nbsp;

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:29.040
One of the biggest challenges for a customer here&nbsp;
isn't actually pricing. It's the switching cost.&nbsp;

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:35.280
If you've used Premiere Pro for, say, 10 years,&nbsp;
jumping into new editing software is very hard.&nbsp;

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:39.920
It can take months to relearn shortcuts, the&nbsp;
way plugins work, the way the timeline works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:44.080
general muscle memory, and I say&nbsp;
this as someone who's done it!&nbsp;

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:48.240
But Blackmagic has made it as&nbsp;
easy as possible to switch.&nbsp;

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:54.160
There's no financial risk in learning it, and&nbsp;
you're not restricted in how much you can learn.&nbsp;

00:13:54.160 --> 00:14:00.080
This also brings me to a conspiracy theory I have,&nbsp;
that Adobe secretly allowed the rampant piracy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.080 --> 00:14:04.720
of their products in the early 2010s.
To get everyone into the ecosystem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:09.680
and build that switching cost.
So when those who pirated software eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:14.400
pay for it, they're already used to Adobe.
Though, I could be wrong.&nbsp;

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:19.200
But going back to DaVinci, this&nbsp;
tactic actually combats piracy.&nbsp;

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:23.840
Why pirate a product that has&nbsp;
95% of its features for free?&nbsp;

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:28.960
The switching cost was as low as possible,&nbsp;
and people were beginning to notice.

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:53.680
In just a few years, it felt like&nbsp;
everyone was giving up Premiere.&nbsp;

00:14:53.680 --> 00:15:00.000
In 2009, DaVinci had just 100 users.
Of course, these customers paid hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:05.760
of thousands, but by January 2019,&nbsp;
DaVinci Resolve had over 2 million.&nbsp;

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:11.840
Blackmagic now had over 1000 staff,&nbsp;
and was making $300 million a year.&nbsp;

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:15.920
For comparison, Apple's Final Cut&nbsp;
Pro had about 2 million as well.&nbsp;

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:23.600
And this is even more true today.
By 2023, Resolve had over 5.4 million users.&nbsp;

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:29.600
Compound Annual Growth Rate (CAGR) is 116%.
And most of that growth happened after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:32.960
free version was introduced.
Blackmagic Revenue also grew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:36.960
to $124 million in 2024.
Though some estimate that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:42.240
by 2025, it was over $550 million.
Adobe's ongoing price increases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.240 --> 00:15:46.960
AI controversies, lawsuits, cancellation&nbsp;
fees, and software stability issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:53.600
keep pushing people toward alternatives.
Hashtags like #DitchAdobe pop up all the time.&nbsp;

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:59.040
Ironically, the best marketing&nbsp;
campaign for DaVinci… is Adobe.&nbsp;

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:03.280
So, the question is: how&nbsp;
does DaVinci really compare?&nbsp;

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:07.680
In the professional video editing software&nbsp;
market, Premiere Pro is still at the top,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:14.800
with 35% market share. Then Final&nbsp;
Cut Pro at 25%, then DaVinci at 15%.&nbsp;

00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:19.840
But, they're growing quickly.
And keep in mind, this is video editing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:23.120
not professional color-grading,&nbsp;
which they are still dominating.&nbsp;

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:29.280
They are slowly eating into Adobe's market.
However, there are two things that concern me.

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:33.120
First is that DaVinci has&nbsp;
introduced a subscription.&nbsp;

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:36.880
But it's a bit different.
What they call the rental model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:41.920
they're offering a $30 per month option,&nbsp;
alongside the full-price purchase.&nbsp;

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:47.200
This has got people a bit concerned.&nbsp;
But, I can see the reason.&nbsp;

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:52.640
Imagine you're a freelancer, and you need DaVinci&nbsp;
just for one job. You can just pay for a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:56.400
month, instead of the full price.
Importantly: The one-time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.400 --> 00:17:02.400
perpetual license purchase is still around.
So for now, I think this is okay, because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:06.640
gives the consumer more choice.
Though there's something else.&nbsp;

00:17:06.640 --> 00:17:11.520
With their new growth, Blackmagic would&nbsp;
be in the perfect position for an IPO.&nbsp;

00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:15.920
That, or activist investors, or private&nbsp;
equity, or anything along those lines.&nbsp;

00:17:15.920 --> 00:17:19.120
I can't imagine what would happen&nbsp;
if investors pushed the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.120 --> 00:17:23.840
to slowly and gradually move to an&nbsp;
entirely cloud and subscription model.

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:28.240
Blackmagic is one of those companies&nbsp;
that fills a gap in the market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.240 --> 00:17:34.480
delivers value, and puts their customers first.
Not only that, they're thinking big picture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.480 --> 00:17:38.880
instead of just the latest trend.
Though with how Grant likes to run his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:44.240
company lean and without borrowing,&nbsp;
I don't think we have to worry.

00:17:46.880 --> 00:18:13.360
Adobe, however, is in a bit of trouble.
Remember that insane revenue graph from earlier?&nbsp;

00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:15.520
Well, take a look at this:&nbsp;

00:18:15.520 --> 00:18:21.040
Despite record revenue and profit,&nbsp;
Adobe's share price keeps falling.&nbsp;

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:27.200
And that's not all. The FTC lawsuit for&nbsp;
their deceptive fees has finally concluded.&nbsp;

00:18:27.200 --> 00:18:32.400
Justice seems to finally be coming for Adobe.
